1. e4
{ White starts with e4, entering the Sicilian trap discussion from White's side. }
1... c5 { Black replies c5, the Sicilian Defense. } 2. Nf3
{ A development move, bringing the knight toward the center and helping control central squares. }
2... d6 { A very common Sicilian setup move for Black. } 3. d4
{ White pushes into the center with d4. } 3... cxd4
{ Black captures in the center. } 4. Nxd4 { White recaptures with the knight. }
4... Nf6
{ Black develops with central influence and simultaneously attacks White's e4-pawn. }
5. Nc3 { White develops, attacks the center, and defends the e4-pawn. } 5...
Nc6 { Black mirrors with development and more central pressure. } 6. Bc4
{ This is the Fischer variation. The bishop immediately eyes f7, a major early weakness in Black's camp because it is usually defended only by the king. }
6... e6
{ Presented as Black's better move: solid, somewhat passive, but it blocks White's bishop and makes direct pressure on f7 much harder. }
( 6... g6
{ This is identified as the mistake that leads into the Magnus Smith trap. Many players want to fianchetto the bishop to g7 for central and dark-square counterplay, but this setup is tactically vulnerable. }
7. Nxc6 { White removes the c6-knight as the start of the tactical sequence. }
7... bxc6 { Black recaptures with the b-pawn. } 8. e5
{ This is the key move of the trap. It attacks the knight on f6 and creates tactical issues around Black's king and queen. }
8... dxe5
{ Black cannot really take this pawn safely. If Black captures with the d-pawn, White has a tactical shot based on Bxf7+ and then Qxd8 because the king was the queen's defender. }
( 8... Ng4
{ If the knight goes to g4 instead of allowing the queen-losing line, White has a strong continuation with e6, shutting out Black's c8-bishop and keeping Black's king exposed. }
) ( 8... Ng4 { Alternative defensive try, but still unpleasant for Black. } 9.
e6
{ White blocks the c8-bishop, maintains attacking momentum, and keeps Black's king dangerously exposed. }
9... Bxe6 { Black tries to eliminate the advanced pawn. } 10. Bxe6
{ White recaptures and continues the attack. } 10... fxe6
{ Black's structure and king position remain compromised. } 11. Qxg4
{ White wins the knight and keeps a very strong attacking game; the black king is exposed and White is much better. }
) ( 8... Nh5
{ A more common retreat for the attacked knight. Fischer's recommendation from here is Qf3, increasing pressure on f7. }
9. Qf3
{ A very important move, adding another attacker to the weak f7-square. } 9...
d5
{ One defensive idea for Black is to block one of White's attackers, especially the bishop's line toward f7. }
( 9... e6 { The other main defensive try is to block the bishop with e6. } ) (
9... d5
{ If Black uses d5 to block, White can still sacrifice because the attack is so strong. }
10. Nxd5
{ White sacrifices/activates the knight, relying on the powerful attack. If Black recaptures with the pawn, White can continue with Bxd5 supported by the queen, and Black struggles to meet all threats. }
) ( 9... e6
{ After e6, White can gain time by attacking the knight on h5 and keep Black tied down. }
10. g4
{ White drives the knight and seizes more space. This also makes Black's intended bishop development to g7 awkward. }
10... Ng7
{ The knight is forced back to g7. This is very passive and blocks Black's important dark-squared bishop, reducing Black's active piece count. }
11. Ne4
{ White continues energetically. The knight pressures d6 and also eyes f6, which would check the king; Black's position is very difficult. }
11... Qa5+
{ Described as a last-ditch try by Black, creating pressure on White's king and e5-pawn. }
12. Bd2
{ Simple development that meets the queen check and sets a trap for the black queen if it grabs on e5. }
12... Qxe5
{ If Black takes the pawn, it becomes another mistake because the queen can be trapped. }
13. Bc3
{ White traps Black's queen on the side of the board and is said to have a very easy game from here. }
) ) 9. Bxf7+
{ The tactical blow. White sacrifices the bishop on f7, forcing the king to move and removing the defender of Black's queen. }
9... Kxf7
{ Forced; the king cannot go to d7 because White's queen controls it. } 10.
Qxd8
